.Dd July 10, 2026
.Dt XTC_XPROC 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm xtc_xspawn ,
.Nm xtc_xproc_register_entry ,
.Nm xtc_xspawn_entry ,
.Nm xtc_xproc_destroy ,
.Nm xtc_xproc_os_pid ,
.Nm xtc_xsend ,
.Nm xtc_xmonitor ,
.Nm xtc_xlink ,
.Nm xtc_xproc_child_main ,
.Nm xtc_xproc_win_child_maybe
.Nd cross-fork spawn, send, and monitor
.Sh LIBRARY
.Lb libxtc
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In xtc_xproc.h
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_xspawn "xtc_loop_t *loop" "const char *name" "xtc_xproc_root_fn root_fn" "const void *arg" "size_t arg_len" "xtc_xproc_t **out"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_xproc_register_entry "const char *name" "xtc_xproc_root_fn fn"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_xspawn_entry "xtc_loop_t *loop" "const char *name" "const char *entry" "const void *arg" "size_t arg_len" "xtc_xproc_t **out"
.Ft void
.Fn xtc_xproc_destroy "xtc_xproc_t *p"
.Ft long
.Fn xtc_xproc_os_pid "const xtc_xproc_t *p"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_xsend "xtc_xproc_t *p" "const void *msg" "size_t len"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_xmonitor "xtc_xproc_t *p" "uint64_t *out_ref"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_xlink "xtc_xproc_t *p"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_xproc_child_main "int ctrl_fd" "xtc_xproc_root_fn root_fn" "void *arg"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_xproc_win_child_maybe "int argc" "char **argv"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
These extend the Erlang-style process relations across a
.Xr fork 2
boundary: a parent spawns a child that runs its OWN xtc runtime, and can
send to it and monitor it as if it were a local process.  This is the
single-host subset of the (unbuilt) distributed design -- a stepping
stone toward cluster support, built over a local socketpair rather than
TCP.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_xspawn
forks a child running
.Fa root_fn ,
which receives a private copy of the
.Fa arg / Fa arg_len
bytes the parent passed.  The parent gets an
.Vt xtc_xproc_t
handle in
.Fa *out .
It must be called from a fiber on
.Fa loop
(the monitor relay lives there).  On a platform without
.Xr fork 2
it returns
.Dv XTC_E_NOSYS ,
because a raw function pointer does not survive process creation there
(a Windows child is a fresh image, not a clone of the parent) -- use
.Fn xtc_xspawn_entry
there, and portably.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_xproc_register_entry
registers a child root function under a NAME in a process-global table.
The same binary -- parent and any re-exec'd child -- resolves the name
to the same function, which is the portable bridge for platforms where a
function pointer cannot cross process creation.  Call it once, early, in
code that runs in BOTH the parent and the child image.
.Fn xtc_xspawn_entry
spawns a child running the function registered under
.Fa entry
by re-executing this binary, which resolves the name in its own copy of
the registry
.Pq Xr fork 2 No and Xr execve 2 No on POSIX, Fn CreateProcess No on Windows .
The fresh image inherits no lock state from the parent, so this is the
form to use from a multithreaded parent.
It requires
.Fn xtc_xproc_win_child_maybe
as the first statement of
.Fn main
(see below); on POSIX, a binary that never calls it falls back to a
plain fork, with the caveat described for
.Fn xtc_xspawn .  It is otherwise identical to
.Fn xtc_xspawn
(monitor, send, destroy all behave the same) and returns
.Dv XTC_E_NOTFOUND
if
.Fa entry
is not registered.  This is the portable and Windows-capable spawn form.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_xsend
sends a byte payload (copied) to the child's root proc; it arrives in
that proc's mailbox as an ordinary
.Fn xtc_recv
message.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_xmonitor
monitors the child from the calling fiber.  When the child exits,
crashes, or its control channel dies, the caller receives a normal xtc
DOWN message (decode with
.Fn xtc_down_decode_ex ) .
The DOWN's
.Va reason
carries the child's exit code, or the signal number if the child was
killed by a signal, or
.Dv XTC_DOWN_KIND_NOCONNECTION
if the channel died before a clean exit was seen.  Internally the child's
fate is mirrored into a local shadow proc, so all the ordinary
monitor/DOWN machinery applies unchanged.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_xlink
is the bidirectional form (like
.Xr xtc_link 3
across the fork boundary): the child's exit delivers an EXIT signal to
the linking fiber, and if the linking fiber / parent process dies the
child observes its control channel close and shuts down.  It links to
the same shadow proc
.Fn xtc_xmonitor
uses, so the intra-process link machinery extends across the fork
unchanged.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_xproc_os_pid
returns the child's OS pid (for logging), or -1.
.Fn xtc_xproc_destroy
terminates and reaps the child if it is still running, closes the
channel, and frees the handle.
A running child is sent
.Dv SIGTERM ,
given up to 2 seconds to exit, then sent
.Dv SIGKILL ;
either way it is reaped before destroy returns, so no orphan and no
zombie remain.
Call it from a process: the wait yields rather than blocking the loop.
Passing
.Dv NULL
is a no-op; destroying the same handle twice is undefined.
.Pp
The DOWN a monitor receives classifies the child's death exactly:
a child that returns or exits with 0 is
.Dv XTC_DOWN_KIND_CLEAN ;
one that exits with a nonzero code is
.Dv XTC_DOWN_KIND_EXIT
with that code; one killed by a signal is
.Dv XTC_DOWN_KIND_SIGNAL
with the signal number.
So a child killed by
.Dv SIGSEGV
and one that called
.Fn exit 11
are distinguishable.
Releases before 1.50 reported every signal death as
.Dv XTC_DOWN_KIND_EXIT
with the signal number as the exit code, and every short-lived child's
status as 96 (a truncated internal NOPROC code).
.Pp
Destroying a monitored or linked child is safe: the monitor still
receives its DOWN, because the internal shadow process that reports the
child's fate holds its own reference to the child's state and releases
it only when it is done.
.Pp
Releases before 1.50 neither signalled nor reaped a running child
(it was orphaned and later became a zombie), and freed state that a
live monitor was still reading.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_xproc_child_main
is the child-side entry that
.Fn xtc_xspawn
arranges automatically; it is public so a re-exec'd child can re-enter
its runtime.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_xproc_win_child_maybe
is wired as the first statement of
.Fn main ,
after the
.Fn xtc_xproc_register_entry
calls, in a binary that hosts
.Fn xtc_xspawn_entry
children, on every platform (the name predates POSIX re-exec).
If the cross-process-child sentinel argv is present it connects the
control channel, receives its arg, runs the registered entry, and exits
without returning; otherwise it returns 0 and normal startup continues.
On POSIX it also records that the binary has the hook, which is what
enables re-exec: without it a re-exec'd child would run
.Fn main
from the top and repeat whatever the parent did.
.Pp
Releases before 1.50 forked the POSIX
.Fn xtc_xspawn_entry
child straight into a new runtime without exec, so a child forked while
another parent thread held one of the library's process-wide locks could
block forever; the hook was a no-op on POSIX then, so binaries that
followed the earlier advice already have it.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_xspawn
still forks without exec, because a raw function pointer is only
meaningful in a clone of the parent.
Its child runs a full runtime in a copy of a possibly multithreaded
parent; prefer
.Fn xtc_xspawn_entry
there.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn xtc_xspawn ,
.Fn xtc_xsend ,
and
.Fn xtc_xmonitor
return
.Dv XTC_OK
or a negative
.Dv XTC_E_*
code (notably
.Dv XTC_E_NOSYS
where
.Xr fork 2
is unavailable).
.Fn xtc_xproc_os_pid
returns the OS pid or -1.
.Fn xtc_xproc_child_main
returns the child's exit code.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_xproc_destroy
returns no value.
.Sh NOTES
POSIX only; the Windows build declines every entry point with
.Dv XTC_E_NOSYS .
.Pp
Because cross-fork spawn uses a real
.Xr fork 2
and
.Xr waitpid 2 ,
it is deliberately NOT reachable by the deterministic simulator (the sim
is single-process and cannot observe a real child's schedule) -- the same
boundary that keeps native AIO and the thread pool out of the sim.  It is
covered by the unit test tier
.Pq test/m10/test_xproc.c ;
a future DST model would run both sides as loops over a simulated
socketpair, as the distributed design describes.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr xtc_osproc 3 ,
.Xr xtc_proc 3 ,
.Xr xtc_net 3 ,
.Xr xtc 7
